"""A Level Pure Mathematics with Mechanics (Upper Sixth): momentum, impulse, collisions, work, energy and power.

KKEVO draft material: six focused lessons for the option "Pure Mathematics with Mechanics". They build on the
Lower Sixth work on forces and motion (Newton's second law, constant acceleration). Unless a question says
otherwise, g = 9.8 m/s^2.
"""

from .dsl import *  # noqa: F403

COURSE = [("pure-mathematics-with-mechanics", "upper-sixth")]

MOMENTUM = "Momentum, Impulse & Collisions"
WORK = "Work, Energy & Power"
T_MOM = "Momentum and impulse"
T_COLL = "Collisions and loss of kinetic energy"
T_WORK = "Work and kinetic energy"
T_PE = "Potential energy and conservation of energy"
T_POWER = "Power and moving vehicles"

LESSONS = []

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 1 Momentum and impulse
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=MOMENTUM,
    topic=T_MOM,
    title="Momentum and impulse",
    slug="meu1-momentum-and-impulse",
    minutes=22,
    summary=r"Momentum is mass times velocity and impulse is force times time; the impulse-momentum principle $I = mv - mu$ links them, with signs for direction and areas under force-time graphs for variable forces.",
    objectives=[
        r"Calculate the momentum $mv$ of a particle and give it a sign for its direction",
        r"Calculate the impulse of a constant force and use $I = mv - mu$",
        "Find a mean force from a change of momentum, including reversals of direction and the effect of weight",
        r"Find the impulse of a variable force as an area under a force-time graph or as $\int F\,dt$",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Linear momentum", "Impulse", "Impulse-momentum principle", "Mean force", "Force-time graph"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"""A footballer in Limbe kicks a ball that is lying still. For a few hundredths of a second his boot pushes on the ball, and then the ball leaves at high speed. The force is large but it acts for a very short time, so we cannot use constant-acceleration formulas easily. We use two ideas instead: **momentum** and **impulse**.

You already know Newton's second law, $F = ma$, and the equations of motion for constant acceleration. In this lesson we rewrite the same physics in a form that handles kicks, bounces and crashes. Treat every body as a **particle** (a point with mass) and every force that acts for a short time as having a constant **mean** (average) value."""),
        H("Momentum"),
        D(r"The **linear momentum** of a particle of mass $m$ kg moving with velocity $v$ m/s is $mv$. It is a **vector**: it points in the direction of the velocity. Its unit is $\text{kg m/s}$, which is the same as $\text{N s}$.", title="Momentum", formula=r"p = mv",
          note=r"Momentum is not the same as kinetic energy. Doubling the speed doubles the momentum but multiplies the kinetic energy by 4."),
        T(r"In one-dimensional problems, choose a positive direction and draw an arrow for it. A velocity in the opposite direction is **negative**, and so is its momentum. Getting this sign right is the most important habit in the whole topic."),
        H("Impulse and the impulse-momentum principle"),
        D(r"The **impulse** of a constant force $F$ newtons acting for $t$ seconds is $Ft$. It is a vector in the direction of the force. Its unit is $\text{N s}$.", title="Impulse", formula=r"I = Ft"),
        T(r"Start from Newton's second law for a constant force: $F = ma$. A particle changes velocity from $u$ to $v$ in time $t$, so $a = \dfrac{v - u}{t}$. Then $F = \dfrac{m(v - u)}{t}$, and multiplying by $t$ gives the result below."),
        KEY("The impulse-momentum principle", r"The impulse of the resultant force on a particle equals its **change of momentum**.", cards=[
            ["In symbols", r"$I = Ft = mv - mu$"],
            ["In words", "Impulse = final momentum minus initial momentum"],
            ["Mean force", r"$F = \dfrac{mv - mu}{t}$ when the time $t$ is known"],
        ], note=r"Always subtract **initial** from **final**, with signs, so the result tells you the direction of the impulse."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a kicked ball", intro=r"A football of mass $0.45$ kg is at rest. A player kicks it and it leaves his boot at $18$ m/s. The contact lasts $0.02$ s. Find (a) the impulse on the ball and (b) the mean force of the kick. Take the direction of the kick as positive.", steps=[
            S("(a) Final minus initial momentum", math=r"I = mv - mu = 0.45 \times 18 - 0.45 \times 0 = 8.1 \text{ N s}"),
            S("(b) Use $I = Ft$", math=r"F = \dfrac{I}{t} = \dfrac{8.1}{0.02} = 405 \text{ N}"),
            S("Answer", text=r"The impulse is $8.1$ N s in the direction of the kick, and the mean force is $405$ N. The weight of the ball ($4.4$ N) is tiny in comparison, so we ignore it for such a short contact."),
        ], check=r"405 \times 0.02 = 8.1"),
        H("Direction matters: rebounds"),
        T(r"When a ball bounces back, the velocity changes sign. The change of momentum is then **larger** than either momentum alone, because you are reversing the motion. This is the point most candidates get wrong in collisions with a wall."),
        EX("Worked example 2: a ball rebounds from a wall", intro=r"A ball of mass $0.2$ kg hits a vertical wall horizontally at $12$ m/s and rebounds at $8$ m/s. The contact lasts $0.005$ s. Find the magnitude of the impulse the wall exerts on the ball and the mean force.", steps=[
            S("Choose the positive direction", text=r"Take the direction **away from the wall** (the rebound direction) as positive."),
            S("Write the velocities with signs", math=r"u = -12 \text{ m/s (towards the wall)}, \qquad v = +8 \text{ m/s}"),
            S("Impulse = change of momentum", math=r"I = mv - mu = 0.2(8) - 0.2(-12) = 1.6 + 2.4 = 4.0 \text{ N s}"),
            S("Mean force", math=r"F = \dfrac{4.0}{0.005} = 800 \text{ N}"),
            S("Answer", text=r"The impulse is $4.0$ N s directed away from the wall, and the mean force is $800$ N."),
        ], check=r"0.2 \times (12 + 8) = 4.0"),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting that the velocity changes sign", r"In the rebound above, a student who writes $0.2 \times (12 - 8) = 0.8$ N s has treated both speeds as positive. The ball **reverses**, so the speeds add: $12 + 8 = 20$. Always write a sign on every velocity before you subtract."),
        H("Weight, and variable forces"),
        T(r"For a very short kick or a bat strike, weight is negligible. But if a force acts for several seconds (a rope lifting a crate, a person landing on the ground), the **resultant** force must include the weight. The resultant of an upward force $T$ and the weight $mg$ is $T - mg$ upwards, so the impulse equation becomes $(T - mg)t = mv - mu$."),
        T(r"If the force changes with time, the impulse is the **area under the force-time graph**. In calculus form, the impulse of a force $F(t)$ from $t = a$ to $t = b$ is $\int_a^b F\,dt$. Either way, the impulse-momentum principle still holds."),
        IMG("apomeu1-force-time.svg", alt="Force-time graph for a particle: the force rises from 0 to 12 newtons in 2 seconds, stays at 12 newtons until 6 seconds and falls back to 0 at 8 seconds. The shaded area is the impulse, 72 newton seconds.", caption="Figure 1. The area under a force-time graph is the impulse: 12 + 48 + 12 = 72 N s."),
        EX("Worked example 3: a variable force", intro=r"A particle of mass $6$ kg is at rest on a smooth horizontal table. A horizontal force acts on it with the force-time graph in Figure 1. Find (a) the impulse of the force and (b) the speed of the particle at $t = 8$ s. (c) A second particle of mass $3$ kg, at rest, is acted on by $F = 6t$ newtons for $0 \le t \le 4$. Find its final speed.", steps=[
            S("(a) Split the area into a triangle, a rectangle and a triangle", math=r"\tfrac{1}{2}(2)(12) + (4)(12) + \tfrac{1}{2}(2)(12) = 12 + 48 + 12 = 72 \text{ N s}"),
            S("(b) Use the impulse-momentum principle", math=r"72 = 6v - 6(0) \;\Rightarrow\; v = 12 \text{ m/s}"),
            S("(c) Integrate the force", math=r"I = \int_0^4 6t\,dt = \left[3t^2\right]_0^4 = 48 \text{ N s}"),
            S("(c) Divide by the mass", math=r"48 = 3v - 0 \;\Rightarrow\; v = 16 \text{ m/s}"),
        ], check=r"\text{(c) } \tfrac{1}{2}(4)(24) = 48 \text{ (area of the triangle under } F = 6t)"),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up impulse and momentum units or masses", r"Impulse is in N s, momentum in kg m/s: these are the same unit. Always convert grams to kilograms first: a $200$ g ball has $m = 0.2$ kg. Also divide by the mass at the end: impulse gives $mv$, not $v$."),
        QUIZ(r"A $0.5$ kg ball hits a wall at $6$ m/s and rebounds along the same line at $4$ m/s. What is the magnitude of the impulse of the wall on the ball?",
             [r"$1$ N s", r"$5$ N s", r"$2$ N s", r"$3$ N s"], 1,
             r"The velocities have opposite signs: $I = 0.5(4) - 0.5(-6) = 2 + 3 = 5$ N s.",
             hint=r"Take the rebound direction as positive. What is the sign of the initial velocity?",
             feedback=[r"You subtracted the speeds ($0.5 \times 2$). The ball reverses, so the speeds add.", "",
                       r"This is only the final momentum $0.5 \times 4$. You also need to remove the initial momentum.",
                       r"This is only the initial momentum $0.5 \times 6$. You also need the rebound."]),
        QUIZ(r"Which of these is a unit of momentum?",
             [r"J (joule)", r"W (watt)", r"N s", r"N m"], 2,
             r"Momentum is $\text{kg m/s}$, and $1\ \text{N} = 1\ \text{kg m/s}^2$, so $1\ \text{N s} = 1\ \text{kg m/s}$.",
             hint=r"Write the unit of $mv$ in kg, m and s, then rewrite the newton in the same base units.",
             feedback=[r"A joule is a unit of energy and work, $\text{N m}$.", r"A watt is a unit of power, $\text{J/s}$.", "", r"$\text{N m}$ is a joule, a unit of energy (or of a moment of a force), not momentum."]),
        TIP(r"Exam link: the Board's report on the option paper names impulse among the topics tested. In Paper 1 (multiple choice, Section B) expect a one-step impulse or momentum calculation; in Paper 3 (the mechanics and probability paper) impulse appears inside collision problems, so draw the positive direction first and write every velocity with its sign."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A cricket bat hits a ball of mass $0.15$ kg. The ball arrives horizontally at $20$ m/s and leaves in the opposite direction at $30$ m/s. The contact lasts $0.004$ s. Find the magnitude of the impulse and the mean force. (Ignore the weight of the ball.)",
           r"Take the direction of the rebound as positive: $u = -20$, $v = +30$. $I = 0.15(30) - 0.15(-20) = 4.5 + 3.0 = 7.5$ N s. Mean force $= \dfrac{7.5}{0.004} = 1875$ N."),
        PQ(r"A crate of mass $50$ kg is raised from rest by a vertical rope. After $4$ s the crate is moving upwards at $2$ m/s. Assuming the tension $T$ is constant, find $T$. (Take $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.)",
           r"Take upwards as positive. The resultant force is $T - mg$. $(T - 50 \times 9.8) \times 4 = 50(2) - 0 = 100$. So $T - 490 = 25$ and $T = 515$ N."),
        PQ(r"A particle of mass $2$ kg moves on a smooth horizontal surface with speed $1$ m/s. A force $F = 3t^2$ newtons, with $t$ in seconds, acts in the direction of motion for $0 \le t \le 3$. Find the speed at $t = 3$.",
           r"Impulse $= \int_0^3 3t^2\,dt = \left[t^3\right]_0^3 = 27$ N s. Then $27 = 2v - 2(1)$, so $2v = 29$ and $v = 14.5$ m/s."),
        PQ(r"A student of mass $60$ kg lands on firm ground after jumping from a wall $1.8$ m high. She comes to rest in $0.2$ s. Find the speed with which she hits the ground and the mean force the ground exerts on her. (Take $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.)",
           r"Falling from rest: $v^2 = 2gh = 2(9.8)(1.8) = 35.28$, so $v = 5.94$ m/s. Upwards positive: $(R - mg)t = 0 - m(-v) = mv$. So $R = \dfrac{60 \times 5.94}{0.2} + 60 \times 9.8 = 1782 + 588 \approx 2370$ N. The ground pushes with about four times her weight, which is why we bend our knees to lengthen the stopping time."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Momentum $= mv$ (a vector, in kg m/s or N s). Choose a positive direction and give every velocity a sign.",
            r"Impulse of a constant force $= Ft$ (in N s), and the impulse-momentum principle says $Ft = mv - mu$.",
            r"When a particle reverses direction, the speeds add in $mv - mu$. This is the usual wall-rebound situation.",
            r"If the force acts for a noticeable time, the resultant must include the weight: $(T - mg)t = mv - mu$.",
            r"For a variable force the impulse is the area under the force-time graph, or $\int F\,dt$."),
    ],
))


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 2 Direct collisions and restitution
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=MOMENTUM,
    topic=T_COLL,
    title="Direct collisions and the law of restitution",
    slug="meu1-direct-collisions-and-restitution",
    minutes=24,
    summary=r"In a direct collision of two particles momentum is conserved and Newton's law of restitution (speed of separation $= e \times$ speed of approach) gives the second equation, so both velocities after impact can be found, including for coalescing particles and for impacts with a wall.",
    objectives=[
        "Apply conservation of momentum to a direct collision of two particles, with correct signs",
        r"State and use Newton's law of restitution with a coefficient $e$ where $0 \le e \le 1$",
        r"Solve the two equations together to find both velocities after impact, and the case $e = 0$ (particles coalesce)",
        r"Use $e$ for a particle hitting a fixed wall or the floor, and recognise when a second collision can happen",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Direct collision", "Conservation of momentum", "Coefficient of restitution", "Newton's law of restitution", "Coalescing particles", "Impact with a wall"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"""Two loaded handcarts meet head-on in a narrow market lane in Douala. A taxi bumps into a parked taxi on the Yaoundé road. A ball bounces on a concrete yard. In each case two bodies meet for a very short time, and then they move apart with new velocities. Our job is to find those new velocities.

A collision is **direct** when the bodies move along the same straight line before and after, and the impact is along that line. We model the bodies as particles on a **smooth** surface, so there are no friction impulses along the line of motion. Collisions where the paths are not along the line of centres are not part of this lesson."""),
        H("Conservation of momentum"),
        T(r"During the collision, body $A$ pushes on body $B$ with some force, and by Newton's third law $B$ pushes on $A$ with an **equal and opposite** force for exactly the same time. So the two impulses are equal and opposite. The momentum gained by one is lost by the other: $m_1(v_1 - u_1) = -m_2(v_2 - u_2)$, which rearranges to the result below."),
        KEY("Conservation of linear momentum", r"If no external impulse acts along the line, the total momentum just before the collision equals the total momentum just after it.", cards=[
            ["Equation", r"$m_1u_1 + m_2u_2 = m_1v_1 + m_2v_2$"],
            ["Signs", "Choose one positive direction. Velocities the other way are negative."],
            ["Check", "The total momentum has the same signed value before and after."],
        ]),
        IMG("apomeu1-collision-before-after.svg", alt="Two blocks on a line, labelled m1 and m2. Before the collision they have velocities u1 and u2 to the right. After the collision they have velocities v1 and v2 to the right. The equations of momentum and restitution are written below the figure.", caption="Figure 1. A direct collision. Positive is to the right. Here $A$ (mass $m_1$) is on the left and $B$ (mass $m_2$) on the right."),
        H("Newton's law of restitution"),
        T(r"Momentum alone gives **one** equation, but there are **two** unknowns, $v_1$ and $v_2$. We need a second equation. It comes from experiment. Write the momentum equation and the restitution equation, with signs, then solve the two linear equations by substitution."),
        D(r"When two bodies collide directly, the **speed of separation** is a constant fraction $e$ of the **speed of approach**. The number $e$, with $0 \le e \le 1$, is the **coefficient of restitution** for the two surfaces.", title="Newton's law of restitution",
          formula=r"v_2 - v_1 = e\,(u_1 - u_2)",
          note=r"Written for $A$ on the left of $B$, positive to the right, so approach speed $= u_1 - u_2$ and separation speed $= v_2 - v_1$. Textbooks write this in different but equivalent ways (for example $v_1 - v_2 = -e(u_1 - u_2)$): check your textbook, and keep the idea 'separation = $e \times$ approach'."),
        KEY("What the value of e means", r"The coefficient $e$ describes how 'bouncy' the collision is.", cards=[
            [r"$e = 1$", "Perfectly elastic: the particles separate as fast as they approached. No kinetic energy is lost."],
            [r"$0 < e < 1$", "Partly elastic. The bodies separate, but more slowly. Kinetic energy is lost."],
            [r"$e = 0$", "Inelastic: the bodies do not separate. They move together after impact (they **coalesce**)."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: both particles moving the same way", intro=r"Particle $A$ of mass $2$ kg moves at $6$ m/s and catches up with particle $B$ of mass $3$ kg moving in the same direction at $1$ m/s on a smooth horizontal surface. The coefficient of restitution is $0.5$. Find the velocities of $A$ and $B$ after the collision.", steps=[
            S("Choose the positive direction", text=r"Take the common direction of motion as positive. So $u_1 = 6$ and $u_2 = 1$."),
            S("Momentum equation", math=r"2(6) + 3(1) = 2v_1 + 3v_2 \;\Rightarrow\; 15 = 2v_1 + 3v_2 \qquad (1)"),
            S("Restitution equation", math=r"v_2 - v_1 = 0.5(6 - 1) = 2.5 \;\Rightarrow\; v_1 = v_2 - 2.5 \qquad (2)"),
            S("Substitute (2) into (1)", math=r"2(v_2 - 2.5) + 3v_2 = 15 \;\Rightarrow\; 5v_2 = 20 \;\Rightarrow\; v_2 = 4"),
            S("Find $v_1$", math=r"v_1 = 4 - 2.5 = 1.5"),
            S("Answer", text=r"After the collision $A$ moves at $1.5$ m/s and $B$ at $4$ m/s, both in the original direction."),
        ], check=r"2(1.5) + 3(4) = 15 \text{ and } 4 - 1.5 = 0.5(6 - 1)"),
        EX("Worked example 2: particles moving towards each other", intro=r"Particle $A$ of mass $4$ kg moves right at $5$ m/s. Particle $B$ of mass $2$ kg moves left at $4$ m/s. They collide directly and $e = 0.6$. Find their velocities after the collision and describe their motion.", steps=[
            S("Choose positive to the right", text=r"So $u_1 = 5$ and $u_2 = -4$. Do not forget the minus sign."),
            S("Momentum equation", math=r"4(5) + 2(-4) = 4v_1 + 2v_2 \;\Rightarrow\; 6 = 2v_1 + v_2 \qquad (1)"),
            S("Restitution equation", math=r"v_2 - v_1 = 0.6\,(5 - (-4)) = 5.4 \;\Rightarrow\; v_2 = v_1 + 5.4 \qquad (2)"),
            S("Substitute (2) into (1)", math=r"2v_1 + v_1 + 5.4 = 6 \;\Rightarrow\; 3v_1 = 0.6 \;\Rightarrow\; v_1 = 0.2"),
            S("Find $v_2$", math=r"v_2 = 0.2 + 5.4 = 5.6"),
            S("Answer", text=r"$A$ keeps moving to the right, but slowly, at $0.2$ m/s. $B$ reverses and moves right at $5.6$ m/s."),
        ], check=r"4(0.2) + 2(5.6) = 12 = 4(5) + 2(-4)"),
        MISTAKE("Dropping the sign of a velocity", r"In example 2, using $u_2 = 4$ instead of $-4$ changes both the momentum and the approach speed. Before you write any equation, mark the positive direction on a sketch and give each velocity a sign."),
        H("Coalescing particles and impacts with a wall"),
        T(r"**Coalescing.** If $e = 0$, the speed of separation is zero, so after the impact the two bodies have the same velocity $v$. Momentum alone then gives the answer: $m_1u_1 + m_2u_2 = (m_1 + m_2)v$. For example, a $3000$ kg wagon moving at $4$ m/s couples to a stationary $2000$ kg wagon: $v = \dfrac{3000 \times 4}{5000} = 2.4$ m/s."),
        KEY("A particle meeting a fixed wall or the floor", r"A wall does not move, so we only use restitution (momentum is not conserved for the ball alone, because the wall gives the ball an impulse).", cards=[
            ["Rebound speed", r"speed after $= e \times$ speed before, in the opposite direction"],
            ["Bounce on the floor", r"The height reached after a bounce is $e^2$ times the height of the drop, because $v^2 \propto h$."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: a ball and the floor, and a ball and a wall", intro=r"(a) A ball is dropped from rest from $1.6$ m above a horizontal floor. The coefficient of restitution between ball and floor is $0.5$. Find its speed just before impact, just after impact, and the height to which it rises. (b) A ball hits a smooth vertical wall at right angles at $15$ m/s. The coefficient of restitution is $0.4$. Find the speed of rebound.", steps=[
            S("(a) Speed just before impact", math=r"v^2 = 0 + 2(9.8)(1.6) = 31.36 \;\Rightarrow\; v = 5.6 \text{ m/s}"),
            S("(a) Speed just after impact (restitution)", math=r"5.6 \times 0.5 = 2.8 \text{ m/s upwards}"),
            S("(a) Height reached", math=r"0 = 2.8^2 - 2(9.8)h \;\Rightarrow\; h = \dfrac{7.84}{19.6} = 0.4 \text{ m}"),
            S("(a) Quick check", math=r"e^2 \times 1.6 = 0.25 \times 1.6 = 0.4 \text{ m}"),
            S("(b) Rebound speed", math=r"0.4 \times 15 = 6 \text{ m/s, directly away from the wall}"),
        ]),
        T(r"**A second collision?** After a collision $A$ (behind) and $B$ (in front) may meet again. If both move in the same direction, a second collision is possible only if $A$ is **faster** than $B$ ($v_1 > v_2$). If $B$ then hits a wall and comes back, it moves towards $A$ and another collision happens whatever the speeds."),
        MISTAKE("Using momentum conservation for a ball and a wall", r"A ball striking a wall does not conserve its own momentum, because the wall exerts an impulse. Use only 'speed after $= e \times$ speed before' for the ball."),
        MISTAKE("Applying e to each particle separately", r"For two moving particles, $e$ relates the **relative** speeds: $v_2 - v_1 = e(u_1 - u_2)$. Writing $v_1 = eu_1$ and $v_2 = eu_2$ is wrong. That rule is only for a fixed wall."),
        QUIZ(r"Two particles of mass $2$ kg collide directly. Before the impact $A$ moves at $4$ m/s and $B$ is at rest. After it, $A$ moves at $1$ m/s and $B$ at $3$ m/s, both forwards. What is $e$?",
             [r"$0.25$", r"$0.5$", r"$0.75$", r"$2$"], 1,
             r"Separation speed $= 3 - 1 = 2$. Approach speed $= 4 - 0 = 4$. So $e = \dfrac{2}{4} = 0.5$.",
             hint=r"Find the speed of separation and the speed of approach, then divide.",
             feedback=[r"You divided $v_1 = 1$ by $u_1 = 4$. That ignores particle $B$.", "",
                       r"You divided $v_2 = 3$ by $u_1 = 4$. The law uses the **difference** of the velocities after impact.",
                       r"You gave the speed of separation, $2$ m/s, as $e$. Divide by the speed of approach to get a pure number."]),
        QUIZ(r"After a collision, particle $A$ (behind) moves at $1.5$ m/s and particle $B$ (in front) at $4$ m/s, both in the same direction, with no wall nearby. Will they collide again?",
             [r"Yes, because $e < 1$.", r"Yes, because $A$ is lighter.", r"No, because $B$ moves faster than $A$ and is ahead.", r"No, because momentum is conserved."], 2,
             r"$B$ is ahead and moving faster, so the gap between them keeps growing. A second collision needs the rear particle to be faster.",
             hint=r"Compare the two speeds. Which particle would have to be faster for the gap to close?",
             feedback=[r"The value of $e$ does not decide this. Only the velocities after impact do.", r"The masses do not decide it either: only the speeds and positions.", "",
                       r"Momentum is always conserved in a collision, so that is no reason for or against a second one."]),
        TIP(r"Exam link: the Board's report on the option paper names collisions among the topics tested, and says some candidates could not handle a collision with a wall. In Paper 3 questions often start with a collision between particles or with a wall, then ask for an impulse or the energy lost (next lesson). In Paper 1 (Section B) expect a one-step restitution or conservation-of-momentum item."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A particle $A$ of mass $5$ kg moving at $4$ m/s collides directly with a particle $B$ of mass $3$ kg at rest on a smooth surface. The coefficient of restitution is $0.2$. Find the velocities after the collision.",
           r"Positive in the direction of $A$'s motion. Momentum: $5(4) + 0 = 5v_1 + 3v_2$, so $20 = 5v_1 + 3v_2$. Restitution: $v_2 - v_1 = 0.2(4 - 0) = 0.8$. Substitute $v_1 = v_2 - 0.8$: $5v_2 - 4 + 3v_2 = 20$, so $v_2 = 3$ and $v_1 = 2.2$. $A$ moves at $2.2$ m/s and $B$ at $3$ m/s, both forwards."),
        PQ(r"Particle $A$ of mass $3$ kg moves right at $6$ m/s and particle $B$ of mass $2$ kg moves left at $3$ m/s. After the direct collision, $A$ is brought to rest. Find the speed of $B$ after the collision and the coefficient of restitution.",
           r"Positive to the right: $u_1 = 6$, $u_2 = -3$, $v_1 = 0$. Momentum: $3(6) + 2(-3) = 0 + 2v_2$, so $12 = 2v_2$ and $v_2 = 6$ m/s (to the right). Restitution: $v_2 - v_1 = e(u_1 - u_2)$, so $6 - 0 = e(6 + 3)$ and $e = \dfrac{6}{9} = \dfrac{2}{3}$."),
        PQ(r"A ball is dropped from rest from a height of $4.9$ m onto a horizontal floor. The coefficient of restitution is $0.6$. Find the speed just after the first impact and the greatest height reached after it. (Take $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.)",
           r"Speed before impact: $v^2 = 2(9.8)(4.9) = 96.04$, so $v = 9.8$ m/s. After impact: $0.6 \times 9.8 = 5.88$ m/s upwards. Greatest height: $h = \dfrac{5.88^2}{2(9.8)} = \dfrac{34.57}{19.6} \approx 1.76$ m. Check: $e^2 \times 4.9 = 0.36 \times 4.9 = 1.764$ m."),
        PQ(r"A truck of mass $6000$ kg moving at $5$ m/s runs into the back of a truck of mass $4000$ kg moving in the same direction at $2$ m/s. They lock together. Find their common speed and the magnitude of the impulse on the $4000$ kg truck.",
           r"Locking together means $e = 0$, so momentum gives $6000(5) + 4000(2) = 10\,000v$. So $38\,000 = 10\,000v$ and $v = 3.8$ m/s. Impulse on the $4000$ kg truck: $I = 4000(3.8 - 2) = 7200$ N s. (Check on the other truck: $6000(3.8 - 5) = -7200$ N s: equal and opposite.)"),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"In a direct collision, total momentum is conserved: $m_1u_1 + m_2u_2 = m_1v_1 + m_2v_2$. Choose a positive direction and sign every velocity.",
            r"Newton's law of restitution: speed of separation $= e \times$ speed of approach, i.e. $v_2 - v_1 = e(u_1 - u_2)$, with $0 \le e \le 1$.",
            r"Solve the momentum and restitution equations together. Check both in your answer.",
            r"If $e = 0$ the particles coalesce and move with a common velocity.",
            r"A ball hitting a fixed wall or floor rebounds with speed $e$ times its speed before, and a drop height $h$ becomes a rebound height $e^2h$."),
    ],
))


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 3 Kinetic energy lost in collisions
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=MOMENTUM,
    topic=T_COLL,
    title="Kinetic energy lost in collisions",
    slug="meu1-kinetic-energy-lost-in-collisions",
    minutes=20,
    summary=r"Kinetic energy $\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$ is calculated from the definition for each particle before and after a collision; the loss is zero only when $e = 1$, a ball hitting a wall loses the fraction $1 - e^2$, and the loss can be used to find $e$.",
    objectives=[
        r"Calculate the kinetic energy of each particle from $\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$ before and after a collision and find the energy lost",
        r"Show that for a particle hitting a fixed wall the fraction of kinetic energy lost is $1 - e^2$",
        r"Find $e$ or a velocity when the energy lost is given",
        r"Explain where the lost energy goes and when no energy is lost",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Kinetic energy", "Energy lost in a collision", "Fraction of energy lost", "Perfectly elastic collision", "Repeated bounces"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"""When two taxis collide, you hear a bang, the metal bends and the parts get warm. Some of the kinetic energy the taxis had before the crash has been turned into sound, heat and the energy of bending metal. That energy is not available to move the taxis afterwards.

In the last lesson we found the velocities after a collision. Now we find how much **kinetic energy is lost**. The Board's report on the option paper names collisions involving impulse and loss of kinetic energy, and notes that some candidates used the kinetic energy formula wrongly instead of calculating it from the definition. This lesson is about doing that properly."""),
        H("Kinetic energy from the definition"),
        D(r"The **kinetic energy** of a particle of mass $m$ kg moving with speed $v$ m/s is $\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$ joules (J). It is a **scalar**, never negative, and the direction of motion does not matter.", title="Kinetic energy", formula=r"\text{KE} = \tfrac{1}{2}mv^2"),
        T(r"For a system of two particles, find the kinetic energy of **each particle separately** and add. Square the speed, then multiply by $\tfrac{1}{2}m$. Do not first add the velocities, and do not use the total momentum."),
        KEY("Procedure for energy lost in a direct collision", r"Use the definition each time.", cards=[
            ["Step 1", "Find both velocities after the collision (momentum and restitution, last lesson)."],
            ["Step 2", r"KE before $= \tfrac{1}{2}m_1u_1^2 + \tfrac{1}{2}m_2u_2^2$, KE after $= \tfrac{1}{2}m_1v_1^2 + \tfrac{1}{2}m_2v_2^2$."],
            ["Step 3", "Energy lost = KE before minus KE after. It must be positive or zero."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: energy lost between two particles", intro=r"Particle $A$ of mass $2$ kg moving at $6$ m/s collides directly with particle $B$ of mass $3$ kg moving in the same direction at $1$ m/s. The coefficient of restitution is $0.5$. We found in the last lesson that the velocities after the collision are $1.5$ m/s and $4$ m/s. Find the kinetic energy lost, and the percentage of the original kinetic energy it represents.", steps=[
            S("Kinetic energy before", math=r"\tfrac{1}{2}(2)(6)^2 + \tfrac{1}{2}(3)(1)^2 = 36 + 1.5 = 37.5 \text{ J}"),
            S("Kinetic energy after", math=r"\tfrac{1}{2}(2)(1.5)^2 + \tfrac{1}{2}(3)(4)^2 = 2.25 + 24 = 26.25 \text{ J}"),
            S("Energy lost", math=r"37.5 - 26.25 = 11.25 \text{ J}"),
            S("As a percentage", math=r"\dfrac{11.25}{37.5} \times 100 = 30\%"),
        ], check=r"\text{the lost energy is positive, as expected for } e < 1"),
        KEY("A check formula (you do not have to memorise it)", r"Algebra with the momentum and restitution equations gives the energy lost directly. Use it to **check** a calculation, but in an exam show the kinetic energies before and after.", cards=[
            ["Energy lost", r"$\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot\dfrac{m_1m_2}{m_1 + m_2}\,(1 - e^2)\,(u_1 - u_2)^2$"],
            ["Reading it", r"It is zero only when $e = 1$, and largest when $e = 0$. It depends on the relative speed $u_1 - u_2$, not on the speeds separately."],
            ["Check on example 1", r"$\tfrac{1}{2}(1.2)(1 - 0.25)(5)^2 = 11.25$ J, as before."],
        ]),
        H("A particle and a fixed wall"),
        T(r"For a ball hitting a fixed wall at right angles with speed $u$, the rebound speed is $v = eu$. Then"),
        F(r"\text{KE lost} = \tfrac{1}{2}mu^2 - \tfrac{1}{2}m(eu)^2 = \tfrac{1}{2}mu^2\,(1 - e^2)", title="Energy lost at a wall"),
        T(r"So the **fraction** of the kinetic energy lost is $1 - e^2$, not $1 - e$. The fraction kept is $e^2$."),
        EX("Worked example 2: a ball and a wall", intro=r"A ball of mass $0.4$ kg hits a smooth vertical wall at right angles at $10$ m/s. The coefficient of restitution is $0.7$. Find (a) the rebound speed, (b) the kinetic energy lost, (c) the fraction of the energy lost, and (d) the magnitude of the impulse of the wall on the ball.", steps=[
            S("(a) Rebound speed", math=r"v = 0.7 \times 10 = 7 \text{ m/s}"),
            S("(b) Energy lost", math=r"\tfrac{1}{2}(0.4)(10^2 - 7^2) = 0.2 \times 51 = 10.2 \text{ J}"),
            S("(c) Fraction lost", math=r"1 - e^2 = 1 - 0.49 = 0.51, \text{ i.e. } 51\%"),
            S("(d) Impulse: the velocity reverses, so the speeds add", math=r"I = 0.4(7) - 0.4(-10) = 2.8 + 4 = 6.8 \text{ N s}"),
        ], check=r"\tfrac{1}{2}(0.4)(100) \times 0.51 = 10.2"),
        T(r"**Using the energy lost to find e.** Sometimes the energy lost is given and you must find $e$ or a velocity. Write unknown speeds $x$ and $y$, use momentum for one equation and the kinetic energy for another. A neat trick is $(x + y)^2 = x^2 + y^2 + 2xy$."),
        EX("Worked example 3: finding e", intro=r"Particle $A$ of mass $2$ kg moving at $5$ m/s collides directly with a stationary particle $B$ of mass $2$ kg. In the collision $8$ J of kinetic energy are lost. Find the velocities after the collision and the coefficient of restitution.", steps=[
            S("Kinetic energy before", math=r"\tfrac{1}{2}(2)(5)^2 = 25 \text{ J}, \quad \text{so after the collision it is } 25 - 8 = 17 \text{ J}"),
            S("Let the velocities after be $x$ ($A$) and $y$ ($B$). Momentum", math=r"2(5) = 2x + 2y \;\Rightarrow\; x + y = 5"),
            S("Energy after", math=r"\tfrac{1}{2}(2)x^2 + \tfrac{1}{2}(2)y^2 = 17 \;\Rightarrow\; x^2 + y^2 = 17"),
            S("Use $(x + y)^2 = x^2 + y^2 + 2xy$", math=r"25 = 17 + 2xy \;\Rightarrow\; xy = 4"),
            S("Solve", math=r"x + y = 5 \text{ and } xy = 4 \text{ give } \{x, y\} = \{1, 4\}", text=r"$B$ is in front, so it must move faster: $x = 1$ and $y = 4$."),
            S("Restitution", math=r"y - x = e(5 - 0) \;\Rightarrow\; 3 = 5e \;\Rightarrow\; e = 0.6"),
        ], check=r"\text{formula: } \tfrac{1}{2}(1)(1 - 0.36)(25) = 8"),
        H("Repeated bounces and coalescing"),
        T(r"A ball that bounces on the floor keeps a fraction $e^2$ of its kinetic energy at each impact, so after $n$ bounces it has $e^{2n}$ of the original. Its rebound heights fall in the same way. With $e = 0.8$, a ball dropped from $1$ m rises to $0.64$ m, then $0.4096$ m."),
        T(r"The largest energy loss for given masses and speeds occurs when $e = 0$ (the bodies join). A $3000$ kg wagon at $4$ m/s coupling to a stationary $2000$ kg wagon moves off at $2.4$ m/s. Before: $\tfrac{1}{2}(3000)(16) = 24\,000$ J. After: $\tfrac{1}{2}(5000)(2.4)^2 = 14\,400$ J. So $9600$ J are lost, as sound, heat and damage to the buffers."),
        MISTAKE("Using the wrong kinetic energy expression", r"Kinetic energy is $\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$ for **each** particle. Writing $\tfrac{1}{2}m(v - u)^2$ for the energy lost, or $\tfrac{1}{2}(m_1 + m_2)(v_1 + v_2)^2$ for the energy of the system, are both wrong. Square the speed of each particle, multiply, then subtract totals."),
        MISTAKE("Saying that e is the fraction of energy lost", r"The coefficient $e$ is a ratio of **speeds**. A ball hitting a wall with $e = 0.8$ keeps $e^2 = 64\%$ of its kinetic energy and loses $36\%$, not $20\%$."),
        QUIZ(r"A particle of mass $m$ hits a fixed wall at speed $u$ and rebounds at speed $v$. Which expression gives the kinetic energy lost?",
             [r"$\tfrac{1}{2}m(u - v)^2$", r"$\tfrac{1}{2}m(u^2 - v^2)$", r"$\tfrac{1}{2}m(u + v)^2$", r"$m(u^2 - v^2)$"], 1,
             r"Kinetic energy before is $\tfrac{1}{2}mu^2$ and after is $\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$. The loss is the difference: $\tfrac{1}{2}m(u^2 - v^2)$.",
             hint=r"Write the kinetic energy before, the kinetic energy after, then subtract.",
             feedback=[r"This squares the difference of the speeds. It is not $\tfrac{1}{2}mu^2 - \tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$.", "",
                       r"The speeds do not add in kinetic energy (that is only for the change of momentum).", r"You left out the factor $\tfrac{1}{2}$."]),
        QUIZ(r"A ball bounces off a horizontal floor with coefficient of restitution $0.5$. What percentage of its kinetic energy is lost in the impact?",
             [r"$25\%$", r"$50\%$", r"$75\%$", r"$100\%$"], 2,
             r"The speed after is $0.5$ of the speed before, so the kinetic energy after is $0.5^2 = 0.25$ of the energy before. So $75\%$ is lost.",
             hint=r"Kinetic energy depends on the **square** of the speed.",
             feedback=[r"$25\%$ is the fraction of energy that is **kept** ($e^2$), not the fraction lost.", r"You used $e$ instead of $e^2$.", "", r"All the energy would be lost only for a ball that does not move after impact, so $v = 0$."]),
        TIP(r"Exam link: the Board's report on the option paper names collisions, impulse and loss of kinetic energy together as one topic. In Paper 3 the usual sequence is: find the velocities after impact, state the impulse (change of momentum) and calculate the kinetic energy lost. In Paper 1 (Section B) a one-step kinetic energy item may appear. Always calculate $\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$ from the definition and show it."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A ball of mass $0.5$ kg hits a smooth vertical wall at right angles at $8$ m/s. The coefficient of restitution is $0.5$. Find the kinetic energy lost and the magnitude of the impulse of the wall on the ball.",
           r"Rebound speed $= 0.5 \times 8 = 4$ m/s. Energy lost $= \tfrac{1}{2}(0.5)(8^2 - 4^2) = 0.25 \times 48 = 12$ J. Impulse (the velocity reverses): $0.5(4) - 0.5(-8) = 2 + 4 = 6$ N s."),
        PQ(r"Particle $A$ of mass $4$ kg moving right at $5$ m/s collides directly with particle $B$ of mass $1$ kg moving left at $5$ m/s. The coefficient of restitution is $0.5$. Find the velocities after the collision and the kinetic energy lost.",
           r"Positive to the right: momentum $4(5) + 1(-5) = 4v_1 + v_2$, so $15 = 4v_1 + v_2$. Restitution: $v_2 - v_1 = 0.5(5 - (-5)) = 5$, so $v_2 = v_1 + 5$. Then $5v_1 + 5 = 15$, $v_1 = 2$ and $v_2 = 7$ m/s. KE before $= \tfrac{1}{2}(4)(25) + \tfrac{1}{2}(1)(25) = 50 + 12.5 = 62.5$ J. KE after $= \tfrac{1}{2}(4)(4) + \tfrac{1}{2}(1)(49) = 8 + 24.5 = 32.5$ J. Energy lost $= 30$ J."),
        PQ(r"A railway wagon of mass $1500$ kg moving at $6$ m/s hits a stationary wagon of mass $500$ kg and the two couple together. Find the common speed and the kinetic energy lost.",
           r"Coupling means $e = 0$. $1500(6) = 2000v$, so $v = 4.5$ m/s. KE before $= \tfrac{1}{2}(1500)(36) = 27\,000$ J. KE after $= \tfrac{1}{2}(2000)(4.5^2) = 20\,250$ J. Energy lost $= 6750$ J."),
        PQ(r"A particle $A$ of mass $m$ moving with speed $u$ collides directly with a stationary particle $B$ of the same mass $m$. Afterwards $32\%$ of the original kinetic energy has been lost. Find $e$ and the speeds of $A$ and $B$ after the collision in terms of $u$.",
           r"Let the speeds after be $x$ and $y$. Momentum: $x + y = u$. Restitution: $y - x = eu$. So $x = \tfrac{1}{2}(1 - e)u$ and $y = \tfrac{1}{2}(1 + e)u$. KE after $= \tfrac{1}{2}m(x^2 + y^2) = \tfrac{1}{2}m \cdot \tfrac{1}{2}(1 + e^2)u^2$. KE before $= \tfrac{1}{2}mu^2$. So the fraction kept is $\tfrac{1}{2}(1 + e^2) = 0.68$, giving $e^2 = 0.36$ and $e = 0.6$. Then $x = 0.2u$ and $y = 0.8u$."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Kinetic energy is $\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$ for each particle. Calculate it from the definition before and after, and subtract.",
            r"Kinetic energy is lost in every collision with $e < 1$, and none when $e = 1$. The lost energy becomes sound, heat and deformation.",
            r"For a particle hitting a fixed wall, the fraction of kinetic energy lost is $1 - e^2$ (and the fraction kept is $e^2$).",
            r"If the energy lost is given, use momentum and an energy equation with unknown speeds, or the check formula, to find $e$.",
            r"The impulse of a wall on a ball is $m(v + u)$ when it reverses, which links this lesson to impulse and momentum."),
    ],
))


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 4 Work and kinetic energy
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=WORK,
    topic=T_WORK,
    title="Work done by a force and the work-energy principle",
    slug="meu1-work-done-and-kinetic-energy",
    minutes=22,
    summary=r"The work done by a force is $Fs\cos\theta$ (in joules); the work done by the resultant force on a particle equals its change of kinetic energy, $\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2 - \tfrac{1}{2}mu^2$, which solves problems on slopes, with friction and with stopping distances without finding the acceleration.",
    objectives=[
        r"Calculate the work done by a constant force at an angle to the motion, using $Fs\cos\theta$",
        r"Find the work done by weight, friction and the normal reaction on a slope",
        r"Apply the work-energy principle $W = \tfrac{1}{2}mv^2 - \tfrac{1}{2}mu^2$ to find speeds, distances and resistances",
        r"Find the work done by a variable force as $\int F\,dx$",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Work done", "Joule", "Kinetic energy", "Work-energy principle", "Work against friction", "Work done by a variable force"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"""A porter at Mokolo Market in Yaoundé pulls a loaded handcart along the road with a rope over his shoulder. The harder he pulls and the further the cart goes, the more energy he uses. In mechanics this idea is made exact: the **work done** by a force measures the energy it transfers to a body.

Work and energy let us solve many problems without finding the acceleration or the time. You give the forces and the distance; the principle tells you the speed. The Board's report on the option paper names work, energy and power as one topic of the mechanics paper."""),
        H("Work done by a constant force"),
        D(r"The **work done** by a constant force $F$ newtons whose point of application moves a distance $s$ metres, with the force making an angle $\theta$ with the direction of motion, is $Fs\cos\theta$. Work is a **scalar**. Its unit is the **joule** (J), where $1\ \text{J} = 1\ \text{N m}$.", title="Work done", formula=r"W = Fs\cos\theta"),
        KEY("Three cases to remember", r"The sign of the work depends on the angle between the force and the motion.", cards=[
            [r"$\theta = 0^\circ$", r"Force along the motion: $W = Fs$, positive. The force helps the motion."],
            [r"$\theta = 90^\circ$", r"Force at right angles to the motion: $W = 0$. A force perpendicular to the motion does no work."],
            [r"$\theta = 180^\circ$", r"Force opposite to the motion: $W = -Fs$, negative. This is the work against friction or a brake."],
        ]),
        T(r"Weight does work only when the particle moves **vertically**. If a particle moves down by a vertical distance $h$ the weight does $+mgh$. If it moves up by $h$, the weight does $-mgh$. On a slope the vertical drop is $h = s\sin\alpha$ for a distance $s$ along the slope. The normal reaction is perpendicular to the surface, so it does no work. Friction opposes the motion, so it does $-Fs$, with $F = \mu R$ if the particle is sliding and the surface is rough."),
        D(r"The **kinetic energy** of a particle of mass $m$ moving at speed $v$ is $\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$.", title="Kinetic energy", formula=r"\text{KE} = \tfrac{1}{2}mv^2"),
        H("The work-energy principle"),
        T(r"Take a particle acted on by a constant resultant force $F$ along its line of motion. Newton's second law gives $F = ma$. For constant acceleration, $v^2 = u^2 + 2as$. Multiply the second equation by $\tfrac{1}{2}m$ and use $ma = F$:"),
        F(r"Fs = \tfrac{1}{2}mv^2 - \tfrac{1}{2}mu^2"),
        KEY("The work-energy principle", r"The total work done by all the forces acting on a particle equals the change in its kinetic energy.", cards=[
            ["In symbols", r"$W_{\text{total}} = \tfrac{1}{2}mv^2 - \tfrac{1}{2}mu^2$"],
            ["Method", "Draw the forces. Write each force's work with its sign. Add them. Set the sum equal to the change of kinetic energy."],
            ["Be careful", r"The change is $\tfrac{1}{2}m(v^2 - u^2)$, **not** $\tfrac{1}{2}m(v - u)^2$."],
        ], note=r"The principle also holds for forces that change, but then the work is $\int F\,dx$."),
        EX("Worked example 1: pulling a handcart", intro=r"A handcart and its load have a total mass of $100$ kg. A porter pulls it from rest along a horizontal road with a rope inclined at $30^\circ$ above the horizontal, with tension $150$ N. The resistance to motion is a constant $60$ N. After the cart has moved $20$ m, find the work done by the tension, the work done against resistance, and the speed of the cart.", steps=[
            S("Work done by the tension", math=r"W_T = 150 \times 20 \times \cos 30^\circ = 2598 \text{ J}"),
            S("Work done by the resistance (negative)", math=r"W_R = -60 \times 20 = -1200 \text{ J}"),
            S("The weight and the normal reaction are perpendicular to the motion", text="They do no work."),
            S("Work-energy principle", math=r"2598 - 1200 = \tfrac{1}{2}(100)v^2 - 0 \;\Rightarrow\; 1398 = 50v^2"),
            S("Solve", math=r"v^2 = 27.96 \;\Rightarrow\; v = 5.29 \text{ m/s}"),
        ], check=r"\text{the tension does about } 2600 \text{ J (3 s.f.), and the cart gains about } 1400 \text{ J of kinetic energy}"),
        IMG("apomeu1-work-incline.svg", alt="A block on a rough slope inclined at 30 degrees. Arrows show the weight mg downwards, the normal reaction R at right angles to the slope and the friction force F up the slope. The block moves down the slope a distance s, and the vertical height lost is h.", caption="Figure 1. A block sliding down a rough slope. Weight does positive work, friction negative work, and the normal reaction no work."),
        EX("Worked example 2: a crate on a rough slope", intro=r"A crate of mass $20$ kg is released from rest on a rough slope inclined at $30^\circ$ to the horizontal. It slides $6$ m down the slope. The friction force is a constant $40$ N. Find the speed of the crate after sliding $6$ m. (Take $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.)", steps=[
            S("Vertical drop", math=r"h = 6\sin 30^\circ = 3 \text{ m}"),
            S("Work done by the weight", math=r"mgh = 20 \times 9.8 \times 3 = 588 \text{ J}"),
            S("Work done by friction (negative)", math=r"-40 \times 6 = -240 \text{ J}"),
            S("Work-energy principle (the normal reaction does no work)", math=r"588 - 240 = \tfrac{1}{2}(20)v^2 \;\Rightarrow\; 348 = 10v^2"),
            S("Solve", math=r"v^2 = 34.8 \;\Rightarrow\; v = 5.90 \text{ m/s}"),
        ], check=r"a = \dfrac{20 \times 9.8 \times 0.5 - 40}{20} = 2.9, \quad v^2 = 2(2.9)(6) = 34.8"),
        T(r"**Stopping distances.** A car of mass $1200$ kg moving at $20$ m/s brakes with a constant resistance of $6000$ N. The work done by the resistance is $-6000s$ and the car stops: $-6000s = 0 - \tfrac{1}{2}(1200)(20)^2$, so $s = \dfrac{240\,000}{6000} = 40$ m. At **twice** the speed the kinetic energy is four times as large, so the stopping distance is four times as long: $160$ m. This is one reason why speed kills on the Douala to Yaoundé road."),
        H("A variable force"),
        T(r"If the force changes with position, the work done in moving from $x = a$ to $x = b$ is $\int_a^b F\,dx$, the area under the force-position graph. The work-energy principle still holds."),
        EX("Worked example 3: force depending on position", intro=r"A particle of mass $3$ kg is moving along a smooth horizontal line with speed $2$ m/s when it passes the point $x = 0$. A force $F = (4 + x)$ newtons acts along the line, with $x$ in metres. Find its speed at $x = 6$.", steps=[
            S("Work done by the force", math=r"W = \int_0^6 (4 + x)\,dx = \left[4x + \tfrac{1}{2}x^2\right]_0^6 = 24 + 18 = 42 \text{ J}"),
            S("Initial kinetic energy", math=r"\tfrac{1}{2}(3)(2)^2 = 6 \text{ J}"),
            S("Work-energy principle", math=r"42 = \tfrac{1}{2}(3)v^2 - 6 \;\Rightarrow\; \tfrac{3}{2}v^2 = 48"),
            S("Solve", math=r"v^2 = 32 \;\Rightarrow\; v = 5.66 \text{ m/s}"),
        ], check=r"\text{the average force is } 7 \text{ N over } 6 \text{ m: } 7 \times 6 = 42"),
        MISTAKE("Using the wrong kinetic energy change", r"The change in kinetic energy is $\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2 - \tfrac{1}{2}mu^2$. Writing $\tfrac{1}{2}m(v - u)^2$ is wrong. The Board's report notes that candidates used the kinetic energy formula wrongly: always compute each kinetic energy separately from the definition and subtract."),
        MISTAKE("Using the wrong distance or component for the weight", r"On a slope, the work done by the weight is $mg \times$ the **vertical** drop, which is $mgs\sin\alpha$ for a distance $s$ along the slope. It is not $mgs$. Remember the normal reaction does no work, and that friction does negative work $-Fs$ while the particle slides."),
        QUIZ(r"A particle of mass $2$ kg moves in a straight line. A resultant force increases its speed from $3$ m/s to $5$ m/s. How much work does the resultant force do?",
             [r"$4$ J", r"$16$ J", r"$32$ J", r"$34$ J"], 1,
             r"Work done $= \tfrac{1}{2}(2)(5^2) - \tfrac{1}{2}(2)(3^2) = 25 - 9 = 16$ J.",
             hint=r"Find the kinetic energy at each speed, then subtract.",
             feedback=[r"You used $\tfrac{1}{2}m(v - u)^2 = \tfrac{1}{2}(2)(2)^2$. The squares are taken first: $5^2 - 3^2$.", "",
                       r"You left out the factor $\tfrac{1}{2}$: $m(v^2 - u^2) = 32$.", r"You added the squares, $25 + 9$. The work is the **change** in kinetic energy, so subtract."]),
        QUIZ(r"A block slides along a smooth horizontal table. Which statement is correct about the work done by the forces on it?",
             [r"The normal reaction does positive work.", r"The weight does negative work.", r"The weight and the normal reaction both do zero work.", r"The weight and the normal reaction do equal and opposite non-zero work."], 2,
             r"Both forces are perpendicular to the direction of motion ($\theta = 90^\circ$, $\cos\theta = 0$), so each does zero work.",
             hint=r"What is the angle between each force and the motion?",
             feedback=[r"The normal reaction acts upwards, at $90^\circ$ to the motion, so its work is zero.", r"The block does not move vertically, so the weight does no work.", "",
                       r"Both are zero, so there is nothing to be equal and opposite."]),
        TIP(r"Exam link: the Board's report on the option paper names work, energy and power among the mechanics topics, and notes that candidates used the kinetic energy formula wrongly. In Paper 3, work-energy questions often have a slope and friction. In Paper 1 (Section B) a short calculation of work done or kinetic energy gained is typical. Quote the principle in words and list the work done by each force before you equate."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A force of $40$ N acts at $60^\circ$ above the horizontal on a box which moves $15$ m along a horizontal floor. Find the work done by the force.",
           r"$W = Fs\cos\theta = 40 \times 15 \times \cos 60^\circ = 40 \times 15 \times 0.5 = 300$ J."),
        PQ(r"A lorry of mass $2500$ kg slows from $18$ m/s to $10$ m/s while travelling $70$ m along a straight horizontal road. Find the total work done by the brakes and the resistances, and hence the average resistance force.",
           r"Change of kinetic energy $= \tfrac{1}{2}(2500)(10^2) - \tfrac{1}{2}(2500)(18^2) = 125\,000 - 405\,000 = -280\,000$ J. So the work done by the resistances is $-280\,000$ J, i.e. they do $280$ kJ of negative work. Average resistance $R$: $-70R = -280\,000$, so $R = 4000$ N."),
        PQ(r"A block of mass $5$ kg is projected up a rough slope inclined at $30^\circ$ to the horizontal, with initial speed $8$ m/s. The friction force is a constant $10$ N. Find the distance the block travels up the slope before it comes to rest. (Take $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.)",
           r"Let the distance be $d$. Weight does $-5 \times 9.8 \times d\sin 30^\circ = -24.5d$. Friction does $-10d$. Work-energy: $-24.5d - 10d = 0 - \tfrac{1}{2}(5)(8^2) = -160$. So $34.5d = 160$ and $d = 4.64$ m."),
        PQ(r"A stone of mass $2$ kg is dropped from rest from a point $3$ m above soft ground. It sinks $0.05$ m into the ground before stopping. Assuming a constant resistance $R$ from the ground, find $R$. (Take $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.)",
           r"Consider the whole motion from release to rest. The weight does work $2 \times 9.8 \times (3 + 0.05) = 59.78$ J. The ground does $-0.05R$. Total work $= 0$ because the kinetic energy is zero at the start and at the end. So $59.78 = 0.05R$ and $R = 1195.6 \approx 1200$ N."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Work done by a constant force $= Fs\cos\theta$ joules. A force at right angles to the motion does no work; a force against the motion does negative work.",
            r"Weight does $\pm mgh$ for a vertical rise or fall $h$ (on a slope $h = s\sin\alpha$); the normal reaction does no work; friction does $-Fs$.",
            r"The work-energy principle: total work done $= \tfrac{1}{2}mv^2 - \tfrac{1}{2}mu^2$. Subtract the two kinetic energies; do not square the difference of the speeds.",
            r"For a force that varies with position, work done $= \int F\,dx$.",
            r"Stopping distance: because kinetic energy depends on $v^2$, doubling the speed multiplies the braking distance by $4$."),
    ],
))


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 5 Potential energy and conservation of energy
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=WORK,
    topic=T_PE,
    title="Gravitational potential energy and conservation of energy",
    slug="meu1-potential-energy-and-conservation-of-energy",
    minutes=22,
    summary=r"Gravitational potential energy is $mgh$ above a chosen level; when only gravity (and forces perpendicular to the motion) do work, kinetic plus potential energy stays constant, and otherwise the work done by the other forces equals the change in mechanical energy.",
    objectives=[
        r"Calculate gravitational potential energy $mgh$ relative to a chosen reference level",
        "Apply conservation of mechanical energy to vertical motion, smooth slopes and curves, and pendulums",
        "Use the work done by friction or a driving force as the change in mechanical energy",
        "Apply energy methods to connected particles and decide when energy is not conserved",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Gravitational potential energy", "Reference level", "Mechanical energy", "Conservation of energy", "Work done against resistance"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A cyclist in Buea reaches the top of a hill and stops pedalling. The bicycle rolls down and speeds up. The height that the cyclist had at the top has been turned into speed at the bottom. This is the idea behind **potential energy**: energy stored because of position."),
        T(r"In the last lesson we used work to change kinetic energy. When the force doing the work is the **weight**, there is a shortcut. We store the work as potential energy, so we do not need to calculate it step by step, and the path (a slope, a curve, a swing) does not matter. Only the **vertical height** matters."),
        H("Gravitational potential energy"),
        D(r"The **gravitational potential energy** (PE) of a particle of mass $m$ kg at a height $h$ metres above a chosen **reference level** is $mgh$ joules. Below the reference level, $h$ is negative and so is the PE.", title="Gravitational potential energy", formula=r"\text{PE} = mgh",
          note=r"Only **changes** in potential energy matter, so the choice of reference level is yours. Pick the lowest point of the motion to avoid negative numbers."),
        T(r"When a particle falls from height $h_1$ to height $h_2$, the weight does the work $mg(h_1 - h_2)$. By the work-energy principle this equals the gain in kinetic energy. Rearranging gives conservation of energy."),
        KEY("Conservation of mechanical energy", r"If the only forces that do work are weight and forces perpendicular to the motion (such as the normal reaction of a smooth surface, or the tension in a pendulum string), then kinetic energy plus potential energy stays constant.", cards=[
            ["In symbols", r"$\tfrac{1}{2}mu^2 + mgh_1 = \tfrac{1}{2}mv^2 + mgh_2$"],
            ["Mass cancels", r"If it is one particle, divide by $m$: $v^2 = u^2 + 2g(h_1 - h_2)$, as with constant acceleration."],
            ["Path does not matter", "Only the vertical heights $h_1$ and $h_2$ enter, not the distance travelled along a slope or curve."],
        ]),
        KEY("When other forces do work", r"A friction force, a driving force or a resistance does work $W_{\text{other}}$ as well. Then", cards=[
            ["Energy equation", r"$W_{\text{other}} = (\text{KE}_2 + \text{PE}_2) - (\text{KE}_1 + \text{PE}_1)$"],
            ["Friction", r"Friction does negative work, so the mechanical energy decreases. The lost energy becomes heat."],
            ["Driving force", "A driving force does positive work, so the mechanical energy increases."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: a ball thrown upwards", intro=r"A ball is thrown vertically upwards at $14$ m/s from ground level. Ignore air resistance. Find (a) the greatest height reached and (b) its speed when it is $4$ m above the ground. (Take $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.)", steps=[
            S("Reference level", text="Take the ground as level zero."),
            S("(a) At the highest point the speed is $0$", math=r"\tfrac{1}{2}m(14)^2 + 0 = 0 + mgh \;\Rightarrow\; h = \dfrac{196}{2 \times 9.8} = 10 \text{ m}"),
            S("(b) At $4$ m", math=r"\tfrac{1}{2}(14)^2 = \tfrac{1}{2}v^2 + 9.8(4) \;\Rightarrow\; 98 = \tfrac{1}{2}v^2 + 39.2"),
            S("Solve", math=r"v^2 = 117.6 \;\Rightarrow\; v = 10.8 \text{ m/s}"),
        ], check=r"v^2 = u^2 - 2gs = 196 - 2(9.8)(4) = 117.6"),
        H("Smooth slopes, curves and falls"),
        IMG("apomeu1-smooth-slide.svg", alt="A smooth curved track. A particle is released from rest at A, a height h = 4.9 metres above the lowest level. At B on the level part of the track its speed is 9.8 metres per second.", caption="Figure 1. The speed at B depends only on the vertical drop $h$, not on the shape of the track."),
        EX("Worked example 2: a smooth slide, and a cliff", intro=r"(a) A child of mass $30$ kg is released from rest at the top of a smooth curved slide. The top is $4.9$ m above the bottom. Find her speed at the bottom. (b) A stone is thrown from the top of a cliff $20$ m high with speed $15$ m/s in **any** direction (up, down or sideways). Find its speed when it reaches the ground, ignoring air resistance. (Take $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.)", steps=[
            S("(a) Energy: the normal reaction does no work, so mechanical energy is conserved", math=r"mgh = \tfrac{1}{2}mv^2 \;\Rightarrow\; v^2 = 2(9.8)(4.9) = 96.04"),
            S("(a) Speed", math=r"v = 9.8 \text{ m/s}", text="The mass of $30$ kg and the shape of the slide do not matter."),
            S("(b) Take the ground as level zero. Start: $20$ m above it", math=r"\tfrac{1}{2}m(15)^2 + mg(20) = \tfrac{1}{2}mv^2"),
            S("(b) Divide by $m$ and solve", math=r"v^2 = 225 + 2(9.8)(20) = 617 \;\Rightarrow\; v = 24.8 \text{ m/s}"),
            S("(b) Direction", text="The answer does not depend on the direction of throw, because only the vertical drop enters."),
        ]),
        H("When friction acts"),
        EX("Worked example 3: a rough slope", intro=r"A crate of mass $25$ kg is released from rest at the top of a rough slope $10$ m long, inclined at an angle $\alpha$ to the horizontal where $\sin\alpha = 0.6$. The coefficient of friction between the crate and the slope is $0.5$. Find the speed of the crate at the bottom. (Take $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.)", steps=[
            S("Vertical drop", math=r"h = 10 \times 0.6 = 6 \text{ m}, \qquad \cos\alpha = 0.8"),
            S("Potential energy lost", math=r"mgh = 25 \times 9.8 \times 6 = 1470 \text{ J}"),
            S("Normal reaction", math=r"R = mg\cos\alpha = 25 \times 9.8 \times 0.8 = 196 \text{ N}"),
            S("Work done against friction", math=r"F = \mu R = 0.5 \times 196 = 98 \text{ N}, \qquad 98 \times 10 = 980 \text{ J}"),
            S("Energy equation: PE lost = KE gained + work against friction", math=r"1470 = \tfrac{1}{2}(25)v^2 + 980 \;\Rightarrow\; 12.5v^2 = 490"),
            S("Solve", math=r"v^2 = 39.2 \;\Rightarrow\; v = 6.26 \text{ m/s}"),
        ], check=r"a = g(\sin\alpha - \mu\cos\alpha) = 9.8(0.6 - 0.4) = 1.96, \; v^2 = 2(1.96)(10) = 39.2"),
        H("Pendulums and connected particles"),
        T(r"A **pendulum** bob on a light string moves on a circular arc. The tension is always perpendicular to the motion, so it does no work, and mechanical energy is conserved. The relevant height is the vertical distance. A bob on a string of length $L$ released from rest with the string at angle $\theta$ to the vertical falls a height $L(1 - \cos\theta)$ to the lowest point."),
        T(r"For two particles joined by a light string over a smooth pulley, add the kinetic energy of **both** particles, and take the change in potential energy of **both**. The string does positive work on one and negative work on the other, which cancel."),
        MISTAKE("Using the length along the slope as the height", r"Potential energy depends on the **vertical** height, $h = s\sin\alpha$, not on the distance $s$ travelled along the slope. In example 3 the slope is $10$ m long but the height is only $6$ m."),
        MISTAKE("Using conservation of energy when friction acts", r"If friction or a driving force does work, kinetic plus potential energy is **not** constant. Add the work done by those forces to the equation, as in example 3."),
        QUIZ(r"A particle slides from rest down a smooth curved track through a vertical drop of $5$ m. What is its speed at the bottom? (Take $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.)",
             [r"$7.0$ m/s", r"$9.9$ m/s", r"$98$ m/s", r"$49$ m/s"], 1,
             r"$v^2 = 2gh = 2(9.8)(5) = 98$, so $v = \sqrt{98} = 9.9$ m/s.",
             hint=r"Equate $mgh$ with $\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$ and cancel the mass.",
             feedback=[r"You used $v^2 = gh$ and forgot the factor $2$ (from $\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$).", "", r"$98$ is $v^2$. Take the square root.", r"$49$ is $gh$. This is not the speed: $gh = \tfrac{1}{2}v^2$, so you must double it and take the square root."]),
        QUIZ(r"In which situation is the mechanical energy (KE + PE) of the particle conserved?",
             [r"A crate slides down a rough slope.", r"A car climbs a hill with its engine working.", r"A bob swings on a light string with negligible air resistance.", r"A parachutist falls at constant speed."], 2,
             r"The tension is perpendicular to the motion and does no work, so only the weight does work. Mechanical energy is conserved.",
             hint=r"Which situation has no force doing work other than the weight?",
             feedback=[r"Friction does negative work, so mechanical energy is lost as heat.", r"The engine does positive work, so mechanical energy increases.", "", r"The speed is constant but the height falls, so the PE decreases and the KE does not increase: energy is being lost to the air."]),
        TIP(r"Exam link: the Board's report on the option paper names work, energy and power as one topic, and conservation of energy is the most efficient method when the question gives or asks for heights and speeds. In Paper 1 (Section B) a one-step energy calculation is typical; in Paper 3 energy methods appear in slopes and in connected-particle problems. State the reference level and the forces that do work before writing the energy equation."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A stone is dropped from rest from a bridge $20$ m above a river. Ignoring air resistance, find its speed as it hits the water. (Take $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.)",
           r"Energy: $mg(20) = \tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$, so $v^2 = 2(9.8)(20) = 392$ and $v = 19.8$ m/s."),
        PQ(r"A pendulum bob of mass $0.5$ kg hangs from a light string of length $2$ m. It is released from rest when the string makes an angle of $60^\circ$ with the downward vertical. Find its speed at the lowest point and explain why the tension does no work. (Take $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.)",
           r"Vertical drop $= 2(1 - \cos 60^\circ) = 2(0.5) = 1$ m. Energy: $mg(1) = \tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$, so $v^2 = 2(9.8)(1) = 19.6$ and $v = 4.43$ m/s. The tension acts along the string, which is always perpendicular to the direction of motion of the bob, so it does no work."),
        PQ(r"A particle of mass $2$ kg is projected up a smooth slope inclined at $30^\circ$ to the horizontal with speed $6$ m/s. Find the greatest height above its starting point that it reaches and the distance it travels along the slope. (Take $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.)",
           r"Energy: $\tfrac{1}{2}(2)(6)^2 = 2(9.8)h$, so $36 = 19.6h$ and $h = 1.84$ m. Distance along the slope $= \dfrac{h}{\sin 30^\circ} = \dfrac{1.837}{0.5} = 3.67$ m."),
        PQ(r"Particles $A$ and $B$ of masses $3$ kg and $2$ kg are joined by a light inextensible string passing over a smooth fixed pulley. Both hang vertically and are released from rest. Find the speed of the particles after $A$ has fallen $1$ m. (Take $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.)",
           r"$A$ falls $1$ m and $B$ rises $1$ m, and they have the same speed $v$. Loss of PE of $A = 3(9.8)(1) = 29.4$ J. Gain of PE of $B = 2(9.8)(1) = 19.6$ J. Net loss $= 9.8$ J. This equals the gain in KE of both: $\tfrac{1}{2}(3 + 2)v^2 = 9.8$, so $v^2 = 3.92$ and $v = 1.98$ m/s."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Gravitational potential energy is $mgh$ above a chosen reference level. Only the vertical height matters.",
            r"If only gravity (and forces perpendicular to the motion) do work: $\tfrac{1}{2}mu^2 + mgh_1 = \tfrac{1}{2}mv^2 + mgh_2$. The mass cancels for one particle.",
            r"If friction, a driving force or another force does work, then work done by it $=$ change in (KE + PE). Friction does negative work.",
            r"A pendulum bob and a particle on a smooth surface have no work done by tension or normal reaction. Use the vertical height.",
            r"For connected particles, include the KE and the change in PE of both particles."),
    ],
))


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 6 Power and moving vehicles
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=WORK,
    topic=T_POWER,
    title="Power and the motion of vehicles",
    slug="meu1-power-and-moving-vehicles",
    minutes=24,
    summary=r"Power is the rate of doing work, $P = Fv$ for a driving force $F$ at speed $v$; with Newton's second law along the road it gives accelerations, the maximum speed on level ground or a hill, and the effect of a change in the engine's power.",
    objectives=[
        r"Define power as the rate of working and use $P = W/t$ and $P = Fv$ in watts and kilowatts",
        r"Write Newton's second law for a vehicle with a driving force, resistance and a slope",
        "Find the acceleration at a given speed and the maximum (steady) speed for a given power",
        "Solve problems in which the power of the engine changes, and recognise that constant power means changing acceleration",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Power", "Watt and kilowatt", "Driving force", "Resistance to motion", "Maximum speed", "Constant power"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A heavy lorry leaves Douala for Yaoundé. On the flat it picks up speed easily. At the foot of a long hill it slows down, even though the driver has not changed the engine. The reason is that an engine has a limited **power**: the rate at which it can do work. At higher speed, the same power gives a smaller driving force."),
        T(r"The Board's report on the option paper names work, energy and power as one topic, and notes that some candidates did not understand how the working rate of an engine changes. This lesson gives you the equations to handle those questions."),
        H("Power"),
        D(r"**Power** is the rate of doing work. If a machine does work $W$ joules in time $t$ seconds at a constant rate, its power is $P = \dfrac{W}{t}$. The unit is the **watt** (W): $1\ \text{W} = 1\ \text{J/s}$. Also $1\ \text{kW} = 1000\ \text{W}$.", title="Power", formula=r"P = \dfrac{W}{t}"),
        T(r"A moving vehicle does work by its driving force $F$ acting over a distance $s$: $W = Fs$. So $P = \dfrac{Fs}{t}$. Since $\dfrac{s}{t} = v$, the speed, we get:"),
        KEY("Power of a driving force", r"The power developed by a force $F$ acting on a body moving at speed $v$ in the direction of $F$ is", cards=[
            ["Formula", r"$P = Fv$"],
            ["Units", r"$F$ in newtons, $v$ in m/s, $P$ in watts. Convert kW to W and km/h to m/s ($\div 3.6$) first."],
            ["Meaning", r"At a fixed power, a larger speed means a smaller driving force: $F = P/v$."],
        ], note=r"$F$ here is the driving force produced by the engine, not the resultant force."),
        T(r"**Equations for a vehicle.** Draw the forces on the vehicle: the driving force $F$ along the road, the resistance $R$ to motion (opposite to the motion), the weight $mg$ and the normal reaction. On a slope at angle $\alpha$, the component of the weight along the slope is $mg\sin\alpha$."),
        IMG("apomeu1-vehicle-forces.svg", alt="A vehicle on a slope. Arrows show the driving force F up the slope, the resistance R down the slope, the weight mg downwards and the normal reaction N at right angles to the slope.", caption=r"Figure 1. Forces on a vehicle moving up a slope. Along the slope the weight contributes $mg\sin\alpha$ against the motion."),
        KEY("Newton's second law along the road", r"For a vehicle of mass $m$ with driving force $F$, resistance $R$ and acceleration $a$ in the direction of motion:", cards=[
            ["Up a slope", r"$F - R - mg\sin\alpha = ma$"],
            ["Down a slope", r"$F - R + mg\sin\alpha = ma$"],
            ["Level road", r"$F - R = ma$"],
            ["Steady (maximum) speed", r"$a = 0$, so $F = R$ on the level, or $F = R + mg\sin\alpha$ up a slope. Then $P = Fv$ gives the speed."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: a car on a level road", intro=r"A car of mass $1000$ kg has an engine working at a constant power of $30$ kW. The resistance to motion is a constant $600$ N. Find (a) the maximum speed of the car on a level road and (b) its acceleration when its speed is $20$ m/s.", steps=[
            S("(a) At maximum speed $a = 0$, so $F = R = 600$ N", math=r"P = Fv \;\Rightarrow\; 30\,000 = 600v \;\Rightarrow\; v = 50 \text{ m/s}"),
            S("(b) Driving force at $20$ m/s", math=r"F = \dfrac{P}{v} = \dfrac{30\,000}{20} = 1500 \text{ N}"),
            S("(b) Newton's second law", math=r"1500 - 600 = 1000a"),
            S("(b) Solve", math=r"a = 0.9 \text{ m/s}^2"),
        ], check=r"\text{at } 50 \text{ m/s: } F = \dfrac{30\,000}{50} = 600 = R"),
        EX("Worked example 2: a lorry on a hill, and a drop in power", intro=r"A lorry of mass $8000$ kg climbs a straight hill inclined at $\alpha$ to the horizontal, where $\sin\alpha = 0.05$. The resistance to motion is a constant $1500$ N. (a) Find the power needed to climb at a steady $12$ m/s. (b) At that moment the power is reduced to $40$ kW. Find the immediate deceleration. (c) Find the steady speed on the hill that the lorry then reaches. (Take $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.)", steps=[
            S("(a) Steady speed: $a = 0$", math=r"F = R + mg\sin\alpha = 1500 + 8000(9.8)(0.05) = 1500 + 3920 = 5420 \text{ N}"),
            S("(a) Power", math=r"P = Fv = 5420 \times 12 = 65\,040 \text{ W} \approx 65.0 \text{ kW}"),
            S("(b) The speed cannot change instantly, so $v$ is still $12$ m/s. New driving force", math=r"F = \dfrac{40\,000}{12} = 3333.3 \text{ N}"),
            S("(b) Newton's second law up the slope", math=r"3333.3 - 5420 = 8000a \;\Rightarrow\; a = -0.261 \text{ m/s}^2"),
            S("(b) Deceleration", text=r"The lorry decelerates at $0.261$ m/s$^2$ (the negative sign means it slows down)."),
            S(r"(c) Steady speed: $F = 5420$ N and $P = 40\,000$ W", math=r"v = \dfrac{40\,000}{5420} = 7.38 \text{ m/s}"),
        ], check=r"\text{at } 7.38 \text{ m/s: } F = \dfrac{40\,000}{7.38} \approx 5420 \text{ N}, \text{ so } a = 0"),
        T(r"**Constant power means changing acceleration.** If the engine power stays constant, the driving force $F = P/v$ falls as the speed rises, so the acceleration falls. You cannot use the constant-acceleration equations ($v = u + at$ and so on) when the power, not the force, is constant. You only use Newton's second law at a **particular instant**."),
        EX("Worked example 3: the power of a hoist", intro=r"A hoist lifts a load of mass $500$ kg through a vertical distance of $12$ m at a steady speed in $40$ s. Find (a) the work done, (b) the power output of the hoist. (Take $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.)", steps=[
            S("(a) At steady speed, the lifting force equals the weight", math=r"F = mg = 500 \times 9.8 = 4900 \text{ N}"),
            S("(a) Work done", math=r"W = Fs = 4900 \times 12 = 58\,800 \text{ J}"),
            S("(b) Power", math=r"P = \dfrac{W}{t} = \dfrac{58\,800}{40} = 1470 \text{ W}"),
        ], check=r"v = \dfrac{12}{40} = 0.3 \text{ m/s}, \quad Fv = 4900 \times 0.3 = 1470 \text{ W}"),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting to convert units", r"A power of $30$ kW is $30\,000$ W. A speed of $72$ km/h is $72 \div 3.6 = 20$ m/s. Using $30$ and $72$ directly in $P = Fv$ gives a wrong answer by factors of $1000$ and $3.6$."),
        MISTAKE("Using the resultant force in P = Fv", r"In $P = Fv$, $F$ is the driving force of the engine. The resultant is $F - R - mg\sin\alpha$, which is what you use in $ma$. Mixing these up is the commonest error in this topic."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting the weight component on a hill", r"For a vehicle going up a slope the driving force must overcome the resistance **and** $mg\sin\alpha$. For a vehicle going down a slope, $mg\sin\alpha$ helps the motion, so it appears with the opposite sign."),
        QUIZ(r"The engine of a car works at $40$ kW when the car travels at $25$ m/s. What is the driving force?",
             [r"$1.6$ N", r"$1600$ N", r"$1\,000\,000$ N", r"$0.000625$ N"], 1,
             r"$F = \dfrac{P}{v} = \dfrac{40\,000}{25} = 1600$ N.",
             hint=r"Change kW to W, then divide the power by the speed.",
             feedback=[r"You divided $40$ by $25$. Power must be in watts: $40$ kW $= 40\,000$ W.", "", r"You multiplied power by speed. Power equals force times speed, so divide.", r"You divided the speed by the power. Force is power divided by speed."]),
        QUIZ(r"A car's engine works at a constant power on a level road with constant resistance. As the car speeds up, its acceleration",
             [r"stays constant.", r"increases.", r"decreases.", r"is zero from the start."], 2,
             r"The driving force $F = P/v$ decreases as $v$ increases. So the resultant force $F - R$ and the acceleration decrease, until $F = R$ at the maximum speed.",
             hint=r"What happens to $F = P/v$ as $v$ grows?",
             feedback=[r"That would need a constant driving force. At constant power the driving force falls as the speed rises.", r"The driving force falls with speed, so the acceleration cannot increase.", "", r"The acceleration is zero only at the maximum speed, not at the start."]),
        TIP(r"Exam link: the Board's report on the option paper names work, energy and power as one topic, and says that some candidates did not have the idea of the working rate of an engine decreasing. In Paper 3 expect a vehicle on a hill with a change of power, or a maximum-speed question; in Paper 1 (Section B) a one-step use of $P = Fv$. Write $P = Fv$ first, then Newton's second law with $F$, the resistance and any slope component."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A crane lifts a load of mass $250$ kg vertically at a constant speed of $0.8$ m/s. Find the power it develops. (Take $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.)",
           r"Steady speed: the lifting force equals the weight, $F = 250 \times 9.8 = 2450$ N. $P = Fv = 2450 \times 0.8 = 1960$ W."),
        PQ(r"A car of mass $1200$ kg has an engine of power $36$ kW. The maximum speed of the car on a level road is $30$ m/s. Assuming a constant resistance, find the resistance and the acceleration of the car when it is travelling at $20$ m/s on the level.",
           r"At maximum speed $F = R$, so $R = \dfrac{36\,000}{30} = 1200$ N. At $20$ m/s: $F = \dfrac{36\,000}{20} = 1800$ N. $1800 - 1200 = 1200a$, so $a = 0.5$ m/s$^2$."),
        PQ(r"A lorry of mass $6000$ kg climbs a straight road inclined at an angle $\alpha$ to the horizontal, where $\sin\alpha = \dfrac{1}{14}$, at a steady speed of $54$ km/h. The resistance is $1800$ N. Find the power of the engine. (Take $g = 9.8$ m/s$^2$.)",
           r"$54$ km/h $= \dfrac{54}{3.6} = 15$ m/s. Steady speed: $F = 1800 + 6000(9.8)\dfrac{1}{14} = 1800 + 4200 = 6000$ N. $P = Fv = 6000 \times 15 = 90\,000$ W $= 90$ kW."),
        PQ(r"A car of mass $1500$ kg is moving at a steady $25$ m/s on a level road with the engine working at $37.5$ kW. The engine power is suddenly reduced to $30$ kW. Find the car's immediate deceleration and the new maximum speed on this road.",
           r"Before: $F = R$, so $R = \dfrac{37\,500}{25} = 1500$ N. Immediately after: $v = 25$ m/s and $F = \dfrac{30\,000}{25} = 1200$ N. $1200 - 1500 = 1500a$, so $a = -0.2$: the deceleration is $0.2$ m/s$^2$. New maximum speed: $F = R = 1500$, so $v = \dfrac{30\,000}{1500} = 20$ m/s."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Power is the rate of doing work: $P = W/t$, measured in watts ($1\ \text{kW} = 1000$ W).",
            r"For a driving force $F$ and speed $v$: $P = Fv$. Convert to W and m/s first.",
            r"Newton's second law along the road: driving force $-$ resistance $\mp\, mg\sin\alpha = ma$ (use $-$ going up, $+$ going down).",
            r"At maximum (steady) speed $a = 0$, so the driving force equals the total opposing force, and $v_{\max} = P/F$.",
            r"At constant power the driving force falls as speed rises, so the acceleration falls; if the power changes, the speed is unchanged at that instant but $F = P/v$ changes at once."),
    ],
))
